workedExamples.question_text:
Use the area model for $24\times37$.
(a) Find the four part-products.
(b) Find the total.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw (2.5,0)--(2.5,3);
\draw (0,1.5)--(5,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.25,3) {$20$};
\node[above] at (3.75,3) {$4$};
\node[left] at (0,2.25) {$30$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$7$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Multiply each row label by each column label.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw (2.5,0)--(2.5,3);
\draw (0,1.5)--(5,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.25,3) {$20$};
\node[above] at (3.75,3) {$4$};
\node[left] at (0,2.25) {$30$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$7$};
\node at (1.25,2.25) {$600$};
\node at (3.75,2.25) {$120$};
\node at (1.25,0.75) {$140$};
\node at (3.75,0.75) {$28$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}20\times30&=600 & 4\times30&=120\\20\times7&=140 & 4\times7&=28\end{aligned}$
(b) Add all four part-products.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}24\times37\\&=600+120+140+28\\&=888\end{aligned}$

